From bbbbcb6619d23b3c1cdab78d997593d9d1f5affc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: baKon <mike.ski83@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:13:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 04/16] kv: every rank carves the least rank's blocks

One block table serves every rank, and Engine::serve refuses ranks whose
paged groups hold different block counts. Under the even split that was
a check that never fired: the ranks' pools had the same geometry and the
same bytes. Under the attention world they do not. A rank with 6 of 16
delta-net heads holds more recurrent state than one with 5 and has less
pool left for pages; the attention-zero rank has no attention weights
and has more. Three ranks over Flash-Next carved 136256, 142352 and
more, and the serve was refused.

KVManager::paged_split is the carve's paged arithmetic with nothing
carved -- the token count the paged groups exhaust at together for a
pool of so many bytes, a windowed group capped at its window -- and
carve_into spends what it says. paged_tokens asks it; cap_paged_tokens
sets a ceiling it keeps under. Engine::configure_kv plans every rank
first, asks each what it could page, and caps every rank at the least
before any carves; a rank that could have paged more says so at info.
At an even split the cap is what every rank would have carved.

